              "id": "980a8b66-2d10-3ca6-926f-cd4650b4d342",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen` function in C++ is used to find the length of a string. The vulnerability arises when the string is empty. In such a case, `strlen` returns 0 and subtracting 1 from it results in a large positive number due to underflow in unsigned integers. This can lead to buffer overflows, memory corruption, and other unexpected behaviors, which can be exploited by attackers.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always check if the string is empty before using `strlen`. Also, consider using safer alternatives to `strlen` that do not suffer from this vulnerability, such as `strnlen`, which takes an additional parameter to specify the maximum length of the string.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```cpp\nif (buffer0[0] != '\\0') {\n    len = strlen(buffer0) - 1;\n} else {\n    len = 0;\n}\n```\n\nIn this version, we first check if the string is empty by checking if the first character is the null character. If it is not, we proceed as before. If it is, we set `len` to 0.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `cstring` library for the `strlen` function.\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "28d296b1-9e25-3cba-9ea9-2126d6e41666",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen` function in C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string until it reaches a null character (`\\0`). If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen` will keep reading memory beyond the intended boundary, leading to a buffer overflow vulnerability. This can result in crashes, incorrect behavior, or even code execution.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that your strings are null-terminated. Be careful when manipulating strings, especially when using functions like `strcpy` or `strcat`, which can overwrite the null terminator. \n\nAlso, consider using safer alternatives to `strlen`, such as `strnlen`, which takes a maximum length parameter and will not read beyond this limit, even if it does not encounter a null character.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```cpp\nbufferlen = strlen(buffer);\n```\n\nYou could use:\n\n```cpp\nbufferlen = strnlen(buffer, MAX_BUFFER_SIZE);\n```\n\nWhere `MAX_BUFFER_SIZE` is the maximum size of your buffer. This ensures that `strnlen` will not read beyond the buffer, even if it is not null-terminated.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strlen` and `strnlen` functions are part of the C standard library, so you need to include the `cstring` header:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d0c9553c-7616-3929-9686-8ed4c8850af6",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc()` function in C is used to dynamically allocate a block of memory. The size of this block is specified in bytes. The function returns a pointer to the first byte of the allocated block. If the function fails to allocate the requested block of memory, it returns a null pointer.\n\nThe vulnerability in this case could be due to the multiplication operation `NUM * sizeof(unsigned int)`. If `NUM` is a user-controlled value and is extremely large, it could lead to an integer overflow. This overflow could result in a smaller block of memory being allocated than expected, leading to a buffer overflow vulnerability when this memory is used.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n1. Always validate user input: Never trust data input by the user. Always validate it to ensure it is within the expected range.\n\n2. Check for integer overflows: Before performing operations that could potentially overflow, such as multiplication, check if the operation will overflow and handle the error appropriately.\n\n3. Check the return value of `malloc()`: Always check if `malloc()` returns a null pointer and handle the error appropriately.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the vulnerability:\n\n```c++\n#include <climits>\n\n// Check for integer overflow\nif (NUM > INT_MAX / sizeof(unsigned int)) {\n    // Handle error\n    return;\n}\n\n// Allocate memory\nunsigned int* ptr = (unsigned int*) malloc(NUM * sizeof(unsigned int));\n\n// Check if memory allocation was successful\nif (ptr == NULL) {\n    // Handle error\n    return;\n}\n\n// Use ptr\n// ...\n\n// Free memory\nfree(ptr);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `stdlib.h` library for the `malloc()` function and `free()` function, and `climits` for `INT_MAX`.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "e284bf3c-ab1d-3021-a4f1-32401c2ff8fb",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc()` function in C is used to dynamically allocate a block of memory. The size of this block is specified in bytes. The function returns a pointer to the first byte of the allocated block. If the function fails to allocate the requested block of memory, a null pointer is returned.\n\nThe vulnerability in this case could be due to the lack of error checking after the `malloc()` call. If `malloc()` fails to allocate the requested memory and returns a null pointer, and the program continues to use this null pointer, it could lead to undefined behavior, crashes, or security vulnerabilities such as null pointer dereferences.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAlways check the return value of `malloc()`. If it returns a null pointer, this means that the memory allocation has failed. The program should handle this error appropriately, for example by cleaning up and terminating, or by trying to allocate a smaller amount of memory.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```c++\nunsigned long long int *ptr = (unsigned long long int*) malloc(NUM * sizeof(unsigned long long int));\nif (ptr == NULL) {\n    // handle error, for example by terminating the program\n    fprintf(stderr, \"Memory allocation failed!\\n\");\n    exit(1);\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc()` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerability Guide](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ad8e05c6-0c1c-3ca5-924a-b5a85cde4a72",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc()` function in C is used to dynamically allocate a block of memory. The size of this block is specified in bytes. The function returns a pointer to the first byte of the allocated block. If the function fails to allocate the requested block of memory, a null pointer is returned.\n\nThe vulnerability in this case could be due to the lack of error checking after the `malloc()` call. If `malloc()` fails to allocate the requested memory and returns a null pointer, and the program continues to use this null pointer, it could lead to undefined behavior, crashes, or security vulnerabilities such as null pointer dereferences.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAlways check the return value of `malloc()`. If it returns a null pointer, this means that the memory allocation has failed. The program should handle this error appropriately, for example by cleaning up and terminating, or by trying to allocate a smaller amount of memory.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```c++\nunsigned long long int *ptr = (unsigned long long int*) malloc(NUM * sizeof(unsigned long long int));\nif (ptr == NULL) {\n    // handle error, for example by terminating the program\n    fprintf(stderr, \"Memory allocation failed!\\n\");\n    exit(1);\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc()` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerability Guide](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "09fa7df6-9da7-346e-9a7a-7a3d8f4a8cd8",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc()` function in C is used to dynamically allocate a block of memory. The size of this block is specified in bytes. The function returns a pointer to the first byte of the allocated block. If the function fails to allocate the requested block of memory, it returns a null pointer.\n\nThe vulnerability in this case could be due to the multiplication operation `NUM * sizeof(unsigned int)`. If `NUM` is a user-controlled value and is extremely large, it could lead to an integer overflow. This overflow could result in a smaller block of memory being allocated than expected, leading to a buffer overflow vulnerability when this memory is used.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n1. Always validate user input: Never trust data input by the user. Always validate it to ensure it is within the expected range.\n\n2. Check for integer overflows: Before performing operations that could potentially overflow, such as multiplication, check if the operation will overflow and handle the error appropriately.\n\n3. Check the return value of `malloc()`: Always check if `malloc()` returns a null pointer and handle the error appropriately.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the vulnerability:\n\n```c++\n#include <climits>\n\n// Check for integer overflow\nif (NUM > INT_MAX / sizeof(unsigned int)) {\n    // Handle error\n    return;\n}\n\n// Allocate memory\nunsigned int* ptr = (unsigned int*) malloc(NUM * sizeof(unsigned int));\n\n// Check if memory allocation was successful\nif (ptr == NULL) {\n    // Handle error\n    return;\n}\n\n// Use ptr\n// ...\n\n// Free memory\nfree(ptr);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `stdlib.h` library for the `malloc()` function and `free()` function, and `climits` for `INT_MAX`.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)"
