            {
              "id": "a8059bff-691a-33aa-a0c8-27f7bf86466e",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. However, it can be a source of vulnerabilities if not used properly. If the size argument passed to `malloc` is not properly validated, it can lead to several issues such as buffer overflow, memory corruption, or denial of service. This is because `malloc` does not initialize the memory it allocates, leaving it filled with potentially sensitive data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate the size argument passed to `malloc`. Make sure it is not too large, which could cause a denial of service, or too small, which could lead to buffer overflow. Also, consider using `calloc` instead of `malloc` as it initializes the allocated memory to zero, reducing the risk of information leakage.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```c++\nmalloc(perThreadSize);\n```\n\nConsider using:\n\n```c++\nif (perThreadSize > 0 && perThreadSize <= MAX_SIZE) {\n    void *ptr = calloc(perThreadSize, sizeof(char));\n    if (ptr == NULL) {\n        // Handle allocation failure\n    } else {\n        // Use allocated memory\n    }\n} else {\n    // Handle invalid size\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `MAX_SIZE` is a predefined constant that represents the maximum size you are willing to allocate. This code checks that `perThreadSize` is within a valid range before calling `calloc`. It also checks that `calloc` successfully allocated the memory.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc` and `calloc` functions are part of the C standard library, so no additional library dependencies are required.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
