            {
              "id": "b132566b-0c99-3c36-ab02-d4a85ae0e773",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc` function in C++. This function is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. The vulnerability arises when the size of the memory to be allocated is calculated using user-supplied or untrusted data. If the multiplication `N * sizeof(int)` overflows, the `malloc` function will allocate less memory than expected, leading to a buffer overflow vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n1. Always validate user-supplied or untrusted data before using it in memory allocation calculations.\n2. Use functions that can handle integer overflows safely when calculating the size of memory to be allocated.\n3. Consider using languages that are not susceptible to buffer overflow vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nA safer alternative to `malloc` would be to use `calloc`, which takes two arguments: the number of elements and the size of each element. `calloc` checks for integer overflow during the multiplication, so it's safer to use when the number of elements is controlled by user input or can otherwise be large.\n\nReplace:\n\n```c++\nmalloc(N * sizeof(int));\n```\n\nWith:\n\n```c++\ncalloc(N, sizeof(int));\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc` and `calloc` functions are part of the C standard library, so no additional library dependencies are required.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)"
