            {
              "id": "a8cb2858-29c9-3c86-8a33-9bc3e7a411a7",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. The vulnerability arises when the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated or calculated, leading to buffer overflow, integer overflow, or other memory corruption issues. In the given code snippet `malloc( N * sizeof(char)`, if the value of `N` is not properly validated, it can lead to these issues.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n1. Always validate the size of memory to be allocated. Ensure that it is within the acceptable range and not a negative value.\n2. Use functions that limit the amount of data written into a buffer such as `strncpy` instead of `strcpy`.\n3. Consider using modern C++ features and libraries that manage memory automatically, such as smart pointers or container classes like `std::vector`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n// Check if N is within acceptable range\nif (N > 0 && N <= MAX_SIZE) {\n    char* buffer = (char*) malloc(N * sizeof(char));\n    if (buffer == NULL) {\n        // Handle malloc failure\n    }\n    // Use buffer\n    free(buffer);\n} else {\n    // Handle invalid size\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `stdlib.h` library for the `malloc` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n\n## Matching CWE\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)\n- [CWE-680: Integer Overflow to Buffer Overflow](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/680.html)"
