            {
              "id": "c93e1810-876e-34f8-aaae-707ebc40d78e",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc(Nbytes)` function in C++ is used to allocate a block of memory of specified size (Nbytes) and returns a pointer to the beginning of the block. The memory allocated by `malloc` is uninitialized and can contain any random data. If the space is insufficient, allocation fails and it returns a NULL pointer. \n\nA vulnerability can occur when the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated or controlled, leading to buffer overflow, memory corruption, or other related issues. This can potentially allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code or cause a Denial of Service (DoS) condition.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n1. Always validate and limit the size of the memory to be allocated. Avoid using user-supplied data directly in the `malloc` function without proper validation and sanitization.\n\n2. Check the return value of `malloc`. If it returns a NULL pointer, it means that the memory allocation has failed. The program should handle this condition properly to avoid null pointer dereferencing.\n\n3. Use functions that limit the amount of memory that can be written, such as `strncpy` instead of `strcpy`.\n\n4. Consider using modern C++ memory management techniques, such as smart pointers, which can automatically manage memory and help prevent memory leaks and dangling pointers.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```cpp\nchar *buffer = (char *) malloc(size);\nstrcpy(buffer, user_input);\n```\n\nUse:\n\n```cpp\nif (size > MAX_SIZE || size < MIN_SIZE) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n\nchar *buffer = (char *) malloc(size);\nif (buffer == NULL) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n\nstrncpy(buffer, user_input, size);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc` function is part of the C Standard Library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n\n## Matching CWE\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)\n- [CWE-680: Integer Overflow to Buffer Overflow](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/680.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "1427ab5c-862c-36e9-a886-222893b7c1a1",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C++ is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. However, it can be a source of vulnerabilities if not used properly. The specific vulnerability in the provided code `malloc(1024)` is that the allocated memory is not initialized, which can lead to undefined behavior or information leakage. This is because `malloc` does not initialize the memory it allocates, but merely reserves a specified amount of bytes.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always initialize the memory after allocating it. This can be done by setting the memory to a known safe value, such as zero. Additionally, always check the return value of `malloc` to ensure that the memory allocation was successful. If `malloc` fails, it returns a null pointer, and any attempt to access such a pointer will result in undefined behavior.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nvoid* ptr = malloc(1024);\nif (ptr == NULL) {\n    // Handle malloc failure\n} else {\n    memset(ptr, 0, 1024);\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `malloc` is used to allocate memory, the return value is checked to ensure that the allocation was successful, and `memset` is used to initialize the memory to zero.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- `stdlib.h`: This library is required for the `malloc` function.\n- `string.h`: This library is required for the `memset` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-908: Use of Uninitialized Resource](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/908.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "e0232c2e-3ce1-365c-9a65-49233e8aad6f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ programming language refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other problems. One such function is `printf`.\n\nThe `printf` function is a common function in C and C++ for outputting text. However, it can be dangerous if used improperly, especially when handling user-supplied input. This is because `printf` can interpret certain special characters (like `%s`, `%d`, etc.) and if an attacker can control the format string, they can potentially exploit this to execute arbitrary code, read from or write to arbitrary memory locations, or cause the program to crash.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using `printf` with user-supplied input. Instead, use safer alternatives that do not interpret format strings, such as `puts` or `fputs`. If you need to format strings, use functions like `snprintf` that limit the amount of output, and always use a static format string.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```cpp\nchar* user_input = get_user_input();\nprintf(user_input);\n```\n\nUse:\n\n```cpp\nchar* user_input = get_user_input();\nputs(user_input);\n```\n\nOr, if you need to format strings:\n\n```cpp\nchar* user_input = get_user_input();\nchar buffer[256];\nsnprintf(buffer, sizeof(buffer), \"%s\", user_input);\nputs(buffer);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code examples above require the following library:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdio.h>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "68ce4e97-e548-3524-8a99-141e4f6244cd",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc()` function in C is used to dynamically allocate a block of memory. The size of this block is specified in bytes. The function returns a pointer to the first byte of the allocated block. If the function fails to allocate the requested block of memory, a null pointer is returned.\n\nThe vulnerability in this case could be due to the lack of error checking after the `malloc()` call. If `malloc()` fails to allocate the requested memory and returns a null pointer, and the program continues to use this null pointer, it could lead to undefined behavior, crashes, or security vulnerabilities such as null pointer dereferences.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAlways check the return value of `malloc()`. If it returns a null pointer, this means that the memory allocation has failed. The program should handle this error appropriately, for example by cleaning up and terminating, or by trying to allocate a smaller amount of memory.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```c++\nunsigned long long int *ptr = (unsigned long long int*) malloc(NUM * sizeof(unsigned long long int));\nif (ptr == NULL) {\n    // handle error, for example by terminating the program\n    fprintf(stderr, \"Memory allocation failed!\\n\");\n    exit(1);\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc()` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerability Guide](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "6abc7b7b-0c37-3ec2-bff1-8251d8ac3981",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc()` function in C is used to dynamically allocate a block of memory. The size of this block is specified in bytes. The function returns a pointer to the first byte of the allocated block. If the function fails to allocate the requested block of memory, it returns a null pointer.\n\nThe vulnerability in this case could be due to the multiplication operation `NUM * sizeof(unsigned int)`. If `NUM` is a user-controlled value and is extremely large, it could lead to an integer overflow. This overflow could result in a smaller block of memory being allocated than expected, leading to a buffer overflow vulnerability when this memory is used.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n1. Always validate user input: Never trust data input by the user. Always validate it to ensure it is within the expected range.\n\n2. Check for integer overflows: Before performing operations that could potentially overflow, such as multiplication, check if the operation will overflow and handle the error appropriately.\n\n3. Check the return value of `malloc()`: Always check if `malloc()` returns a null pointer and handle the error appropriately.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the vulnerability:\n\n```c++\n#include <climits>\n\n// Check for integer overflow\nif (NUM > INT_MAX / sizeof(unsigned int)) {\n    // Handle error\n    return;\n}\n\n// Allocate memory\nunsigned int* ptr = (unsigned int*) malloc(NUM * sizeof(unsigned int));\n\n// Check if memory allocation was successful\nif (ptr == NULL) {\n    // Handle error\n    return;\n}\n\n// Use ptr\n// ...\n\n// Free memory\nfree(ptr);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `stdlib.h` library for the `malloc()` function and `free()` function, and `climits` for `INT_MAX`.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
