            {
              "id": "a977feed-807f-383c-8396-2fcb2dd46b78",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as 'Likely Vulnerable: malloc result used without null check (possible null deref)', which indicates a high-confidence, actionable issue. The malloc call assigns its result to 'hOutputData' and is immediately used as the destination in a memset call on the next line, without any check for a NULL return value. There is no zero guard or overflow guard present, and the first use is a memory operation (memset) that will dereference the pointer. The absence of a null guard before use increases the risk of a null pointer dereference if malloc fails. The presence of a cast does not mitigate the risk. The pointer is freed in the method and passed to another call, but these do not affect the immediate dereference risk. Based on these factors, this is a genuine vulnerability.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent a null pointer dereference after a failed memory allocation, always check the result of malloc before using the pointer. For example, if your original code is:\n\n```c\nhOutputData = (type *)malloc(size);\nmemset(hOutputData, 0, size);\n```\n\nYou should add a null check before the memset call:\n\n```c\nhOutputData = (type *)malloc(size);\nif (hOutputData == NULL) {\n    // Handle allocation failure, e.g., return error or cleanup\n    // fprintf(stderr, \"Memory allocation failed\\n\");\n    return;\n}\nmemset(hOutputData, 0, size);\n```\n\nThis ensures that if malloc fails and returns NULL, the program does not attempt to dereference a null pointer, which would lead to undefined behavior or a crash.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "18c78b27-661b-3cd6-a094-636519d3f6d2",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc` function in C++. This function is used to allocate a block of memory of a specified size. The vulnerability arises when the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated or calculated, leading to a potential buffer overflow or underflow. In the given code snippet, `malloc(N * sizeof(float))`, if the value of `N` is not properly controlled, it can lead to an integer overflow, resulting in a smaller buffer being allocated than expected. This can lead to subsequent buffer overflow vulnerabilities when the buffer is used.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n1. Always validate and sanitize the input values that are used to calculate the size of the memory to be allocated.\n2. Use functions that limit the size of the memory to be allocated to prevent integer overflows.\n3. Consider using languages that automatically manage memory for you to prevent these types of vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nA simple fix for the given code snippet would be to check if the multiplication operation would result in an overflow before calling `malloc`. Here is an example of how you can do this:\n\n```cpp\n#include <climits>\n\nif (N > 0 && N <= (INT_MAX / sizeof(float))) {\n    float* array = (float*) malloc(N * sizeof(float));\n    if (array == NULL) {\n        // Handle error\n    }\n    // Use array\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, we first check if `N` is within a safe range to prevent an integer overflow. Then, we check if `malloc` was successful in allocating the memory.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `cstdlib` library for the `malloc` function and `climits` for `INT_MAX`.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "fd379c8e-e8e3-339c-b3a2-fc103682ea7f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc` function in C++. This function is used to allocate a block of memory of a specified size. The vulnerability arises when the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated or calculated, leading to a potential buffer overflow or underflow. In the given code snippet, `malloc(N * sizeof(float))`, if the value of `N` is not properly controlled, it can lead to an integer overflow, resulting in a smaller buffer being allocated than expected. This can lead to subsequent buffer overflow vulnerabilities when the buffer is used.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n1. Always validate and sanitize the input values that are used to calculate the size of the memory to be allocated.\n2. Use functions that limit the size of the memory to be allocated to prevent integer overflows.\n3. Consider using languages that automatically manage memory for you to prevent these types of vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nA simple fix for the given code snippet would be to check if the multiplication operation would result in an overflow before calling `malloc`. Here is an example of how you can do this:\n\n```cpp\n#include <climits>\n\nif (N > 0 && N <= (INT_MAX / sizeof(float))) {\n    float* array = (float*) malloc(N * sizeof(float));\n    if (array == NULL) {\n        // Handle error\n    }\n    // Use array\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, we first check if `N` is within a safe range to prevent an integer overflow. Then, we check if `malloc` was successful in allocating the memory.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `cstdlib` library for the `malloc` function and `climits` for `INT_MAX`.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)"
