            {
              "id": "17c08522-78c4-367c-b799-b45238fd1404",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `strlen` function in C++. The `strlen` function is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if not used properly. In the provided code snippet, `strlen(name) + 1` is used to allocate memory for `handle->name`. If the length of `name` is not properly checked, it can lead to buffer overflow, as more data than allocated can be written into `handle->name`.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to always check the length of the string before using it in memory allocation. Also, consider using safer functions that limit the amount of data written to the buffer, such as `strncpy` instead of `strcpy`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer version of the code:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t length = strlen(name);\nif (length > MAX_LENGTH) {\n    // Handle error\n} else {\n    handle->name = new char[length + 1];\n    strncpy(handle->name, name, length);\n    handle->name[length] = '\\0'; // Ensure null termination\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `MAX_LENGTH` is a predefined constant that represents the maximum allowed length for `name`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `<cstring>`: This library provides the `strlen` and `strncpy` functions.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "3923f526-bf89-3e48-97a1-fb01b072760c",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen` function in C and C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string until it reaches a null character (`\\0`). If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen` will continue reading memory until it encounters a null character, potentially leading to buffer overflows, memory corruption, and other undefined behaviors.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that your strings are null-terminated. Avoid using `strlen` on strings that come from untrusted sources without validation. Consider using safer alternatives such as `strnlen`, which takes a maximum length parameter and will not read past this length, even if it does not encounter a null character.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```c++\nlen = strlen(name);\n```\n\nYou could use:\n\n```c++\nlen = strnlen(name, MAX_LEN);\n```\n\nWhere `MAX_LEN` is the maximum expected length of the string. This ensures that `strnlen` will not read past `MAX_LEN` characters, even if it does not encounter a null character.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strlen` and `strnlen` functions are part of the C standard library, so you need to include the `cstring` header:\n\n```c++\n#include <cstring>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "2f7ffec1-a36a-33bc-b4cd-0d792ca81a1e",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the call to the string copy function. The destination and source may refer to overlapping memory, which is not allowed by the standard for this function and can result in unpredictable program behavior. Additionally, there is no explicit check to ensure that the source is not null, which further increases the risk. The destination buffer is confirmed to be non-null before the call, but the source is not. No explicit null-terminator is set after the copy, which could also lead to string handling issues. The combination of these factors makes this a high-confidence, actionable issue that should be addressed to prevent undefined behavior and potential security vulnerabilities.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo remediate the risk of undefined behavior caused by possible overlapping memory regions in the string copy operation, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap before performing the copy. If overlap is possible, use a memory-safe function that supports overlapping regions, such as `memmove`, instead of the standard string copy function. Additionally, always check that the source pointer is not null before copying, and explicitly null-terminate the destination buffer if required by your logic.\n\nExample fix:\n\n```cpp\nif (name != nullptr && servaddr.sun_path != name) {\n    strncpy(servaddr.sun_path, name, len);\n    // Optionally, ensure null-termination if len may be less than the buffer size\n    servaddr.sun_path[len - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\n\nIf overlap cannot be ruled out, use:\n\n```cpp\nif (name != nullptr) {\n    memmove(servaddr.sun_path, name, len);\n    // Optionally, ensure null-termination\n    servaddr.sun_path[len - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "6a51c2ad-03f4-397c-8ee0-ae5766b2ac39",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as 'Likely Vulnerable: malloc result used without null check (possible null deref)', and the call verdict is 'GENUINE', indicating a high-confidence, actionable issue. The malloc result (hOData) is used via dereference on the very next line without any check for NULL, and there is no zero guard or overflow guard present. The presence of a cast does not mitigate the risk. The pointer is freed in the method and also passed to another call, but these do not address the immediate risk of dereferencing a potentially NULL pointer if malloc fails. This pattern is a common source of null pointer dereference vulnerabilities in C/C++ code.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nAlways check the result of malloc for NULL before dereferencing or using the allocated memory. If malloc fails, handle the error appropriately (e.g., by returning an error code, cleaning up, or aborting the operation).\n\n**Remediation example:**\n\n```c\nhOData = (type *)malloc(memSize);\nif (hOData == NULL) {\n    // Handle allocation failure: log, cleanup, return error, etc.\n    return false; // or appropriate error handling\n}\n// Safe to use hOData here\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ab9644dc-3c45-3a26-860d-a6fd46da06b9",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as 'Likely Vulnerable: malloc result used without null check (possible null deref)', and the call verdict is 'GENUINE', indicating a high-confidence, actionable issue. The malloc call assigns its result to 'hOData' and is immediately followed by a dereference on the next line, with no null pointer guard present. There are no zero guards or overflow checks, and the allocation size is not a simple literal or sizeof pattern, making it harder to reason about safety. The presence of a cast does not mitigate the risk. Although the pointer is freed in the method and passed to another call, the lack of a null check before dereferencing the result of malloc is a well-known source of null pointer dereference vulnerabilities, especially if the allocation fails. This is a genuine vulnerability and not a false positive.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nAlways check the result of malloc for NULL before using the allocated memory. If malloc fails, handle the error appropriately (e.g., by returning an error code, cleaning up, or aborting). For example, if your original code is:\n\n```c\nhOData = (T*)malloc(memSize);\n// ... immediately dereference hOData\nhOData[0] = ...;\n```\n\nA safe remediation would be:\n\n```c\nhOData = (T*)malloc(memSize);\nif (hOData == NULL) {\n    // Handle allocation failure: log, cleanup, return error, etc.\n    return false;\n}\n// Safe to use hOData now\nhOData[0] = ...;\n```\n\nThis ensures that you do not dereference a NULL pointer if the allocation fails, preventing undefined behavior and potential crashes.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "4ee21db9-afff-33bf-8674-575bf52e20ce",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. The vulnerability arises when the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated or calculated, leading to buffer overflow, memory corruption, or other related issues. In the given code snippet, `malloc(sizeof(int))`, there is no immediate vulnerability. However, if the size of the memory to be allocated is determined based on user input or other untrusted data, it can lead to vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n1. Always validate and sanitize the size of the memory to be allocated. Never trust user input or other untrusted data.\n2. Consider using functions that limit the amount of memory allocated, such as `calloc`.\n3. Use modern C++ features like smart pointers (`std::unique_ptr`, `std::shared_ptr`) to manage dynamic memory, which can help prevent memory leaks and other related issues.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIf the size of the memory to be allocated is determined based on user input, validate and sanitize the input. For example:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nint* allocateArray(int size) {\n    if (size <= 0) {\n        // Handle error\n        return NULL;\n    }\n\n    int* array = (int*) malloc(size * sizeof(int));\n    if (array == NULL) {\n        // Handle error\n        return NULL;\n    }\n\n    return array;\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional library dependencies are required.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "faf39c51-a4d3-3364-bcfb-1bc59cd763ff",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. The vulnerability arises when the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated or calculated, leading to buffer overflow, memory corruption, or other related issues. In the given code snippet, `malloc(sizeof(int))`, there is no immediate vulnerability. However, if the size of the memory to be allocated is determined based on user input or other untrusted data, it can lead to vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n1. Always validate and sanitize the size of the memory to be allocated. Never trust user input or other untrusted data.\n2. Consider using functions that limit the amount of memory allocated, such as `calloc`.\n3. Use modern C++ features like smart pointers (`std::unique_ptr`, `std::shared_ptr`) to manage dynamic memory, which can help prevent memory leaks and other related issues.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIf the size of the memory to be allocated is determined based on user input, validate and sanitize the input. For example:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nint* allocateArray(int size) {\n    if (size <= 0) {\n        // Handle error\n        return NULL;\n    }\n\n    int* array = (int*) malloc(size * sizeof(int));\n    if (array == NULL) {\n        // Handle error\n        return NULL;\n    }\n\n    return array;\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional library dependencies are required.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "2e3fc1f0-71ce-3243-98b4-e007a589d26c",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as 'Likely Vulnerable' because the result of the malloc call is used (dereferenced) immediately after allocation without checking if the allocation succeeded (i.e., without verifying that the returned pointer is not NULL). There is no zero guard or overflow guard present, and the first use is a direct dereference, which is considered dangerous. Additionally, there is no null guard before the first use, increasing the risk of a null pointer dereference if malloc fails. The presence of a cast does not mitigate the risk. These factors indicate a genuine risk of null pointer dereference, which can lead to undefined behavior or program crashes.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nAlways check the result of malloc before using the allocated memory to prevent null pointer dereference. For example, if your original code is:\n\n```c\nhOData = (T*)malloc(memSize);\n// Immediately using hOData, e.g. hOData[0] = ...\n```\n\nYou should add a null check after the allocation and handle the error appropriately:\n\n```c\nhOData = (T*)malloc(memSize);\nif (hOData == NULL) {\n    // Handle allocation failure, e.g., return error, cleanup, or exit\n    fprintf(stderr, \"Memory allocation failed\\n\");\n    return false;\n}\n// Safe to use hOData now\n```\n\nThis ensures that if malloc fails and returns NULL, your program will not attempt to dereference a null pointer, avoiding undefined behavior or crashes.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d21f9216-6d64-3da2-8a0d-c301246a3b8d",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as 'Likely Vulnerable: malloc result used without null check (possible null deref)', and the call verdict is 'GENUINE', indicating a high-confidence, actionable issue. The malloc call assigns its result to 'hOData', which is then dereferenced on the next line without any check for a NULL return value. There is no zero guard or overflow guard present, and the first use is a direct dereference, which is considered dangerous. The presence of a cast does not mitigate the risk. Although the pointer is freed in the method and passed to another call, these do not address the core issue of a missing NULL check. Therefore, this is a genuine vulnerability with a low probability of being a false positive.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nAlways check the result of `malloc` for NULL before using the allocated memory. If the allocation fails, handle the error appropriately (e.g., by returning an error code, cleaning up, or aborting the operation).\n\n**Remediation Example:**\n\n```c\nhOData = (type *)malloc(memSize);\nif (hOData == NULL) {\n    // Handle allocation failure: log, cleanup, return error, etc.\n    return false; // or appropriate error handling\n}\n// Safe to use hOData here\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f7c181a6-b712-3d21-bb65-658ab99c0570",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Prohibited C Function Identified\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, and other security issues. In this case, the `std::getenv` function is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities. This function returns a pointer to the value in the environment, or NULL if there is no match. The returned pointer might be invalidated or the associated string might be overwritten by a subsequent call to `std::getenv`, `std::setenv`, `std::putenv`, or `std::unsetenv`.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using `std::getenv` function directly. Instead, use safer alternatives that are less prone to security vulnerabilities. Always validate and sanitize the input and output of these functions. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `std::getenv`, consider using a safer alternative. For example, you can use the `std::getenv` function in combination with `std::optional` to safely retrieve environment variables:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdlib>\n#include <optional>\n#include <string>\n\nstd::optional<std::string> get_env(const char* name) {\n    if(const char* env_p = std::getenv(name)) {\n        return std::string{env_p};\n    }\n    return {};\n}\n\n// Usage\nauto val = get_env(kEnvarName);\nif(val.has_value()) {\n    // Use val.value() safely\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdlib>`: This header defines several general purpose functions, including dynamic memory management, random number generation, communication with the environment, integer arithmetics, searching, sorting and converting.\n\n- `<optional>`: This header file contains template class optional and its supporting functions.\n\n- `<string>`: This header file contains std::string class and its supporting functions.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "753b7363-a41d-353f-96a9-2d9b1fecf609",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ programming language refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other problems. One such function is `printf`.\n\nThe `printf` function is a common function in C and C++ for outputting text. However, it can be dangerous if used improperly, especially when handling user-supplied input. This is because `printf` can interpret certain special characters (like `%s`, `%d`, etc.) and if an attacker can control the format string, they can potentially exploit this to execute arbitrary code, read from or write to arbitrary memory locations, or cause the program to crash.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using `printf` with user-supplied input. Instead, use safer alternatives that do not interpret format strings, such as `puts` or `fputs`. If you need to format strings, use functions like `snprintf` that limit the amount of output, and always use a static format string.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```cpp\nchar* user_input = get_user_input();\nprintf(user_input);\n```\n\nUse:\n\n```cpp\nchar* user_input = get_user_input();\nputs(user_input);\n```\n\nOr, if you need to format strings:\n\n```cpp\nchar* user_input = get_user_input();\nchar buffer[256];\nsnprintf(buffer, sizeof(buffer), \"%s\", user_input);\nputs(buffer);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code examples above require the following library:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdio.h>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "df2cf880-a3c8-34b8-b593-57d72933ff54",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflows. In this case, the `vfprintf` function is used, which can lead to format string vulnerabilities if the format string is not controlled by the programmer or if it can be manipulated by an attacker.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions, such as the ones provided by the C++ Standard Library. In this case, you could use `std::vfprintf` with a format string that you control, so it cannot be manipulated by an attacker.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer alternative using `std::vfprintf`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdarg>\n#include <cstdio>\n\nvoid safe_vfprintf(const char* fmt, ...) {\n    va_list ap;\n    va_start(ap, fmt);\n    std::vfprintf(stderr, fmt, ap);\n    va_end(ap);\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdarg>`: for handling variable argument lists.\n- `<cstdio>`: for `std::vfprintf` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "171e9d63-e2a8-3cb8-86bf-08029bb1ba58",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ programming language refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other problems. These functions are often prohibited in secure coding standards. One such function is `fprintf`.\n\nThe `fprintf` function can lead to vulnerabilities if not used correctly. It can cause format string vulnerabilities if the format string is not a constant, or if it's composed dynamically, it can lead to buffer overflows if the output is larger than the buffer.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to `fprintf` such as `snprintf` or `vsnprintf`. These functions include the size of the buffer as an argument, which can prevent buffer overflows.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `fprintf`, use `snprintf` or `vsnprintf`. Here is an example:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdio.h>\n\nvoid safe_function(const char* format, ...) {\n    char buffer[1024];\n    va_list args;\n    va_start(args, format);\n    vsnprintf(buffer, sizeof(buffer), format, args);\n    va_end(args);\n    // Now buffer contains the formatted string.\n    // You can write it to file with fwrite or similar\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- stdio.h\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n\n## CWE\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "60b8c391-65d1-3df6-a6ae-d480480402fc",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C++ is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. However, it can be a source of vulnerabilities if not used correctly. If the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated or calculated, it can lead to buffer overflows, memory leaks, or other unexpected behavior. This can potentially be exploited by an attacker to execute arbitrary code or cause a denial of service.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate and correctly calculate the size of the memory to be allocated. Avoid using `malloc` with user-supplied or untrusted input without proper validation. Also, always check the return value of `malloc` to ensure that the memory allocation was successful.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is an example of how to correctly use `malloc`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nstruct TestType {\n    int a;\n    double b;\n};\n\nint main() {\n    TestType* ptr = (TestType*) malloc(sizeof(TestType));\n    if (ptr == NULL) {\n        // Handle memory allocation failure\n        return 1;\n    }\n\n    // Use ptr...\n\n    // Don't forget to free the allocated memory\n    free(ptr);\n\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, we allocate memory for a `TestType` object using `malloc` and check if the memory allocation was successful by comparing the returned pointer to `NULL`. If `malloc` fails, it returns `NULL` and we handle this case appropriately. Finally, we free the allocated memory using `free`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `stdlib.h` library for the `malloc` and `free` functions.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f1be6177-9219-338b-a82a-b9bc494766ce",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C++ is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. However, it can be a source of vulnerabilities if not used correctly. If the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated or calculated, it can lead to buffer overflows, memory leaks, or other unexpected behavior. This can potentially be exploited by an attacker to execute arbitrary code or cause a denial of service.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate and correctly calculate the size of the memory to be allocated. Avoid using `malloc` with user-supplied or untrusted input without proper validation. Also, always check the return value of `malloc` to ensure that the memory allocation was successful.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is an example of how to correctly use `malloc`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nstruct TestType {\n    int a;\n    double b;\n};\n\nint main() {\n    TestType* ptr = (TestType*) malloc(sizeof(TestType));\n    if (ptr == NULL) {\n        // Handle memory allocation failure\n        return 1;\n    }\n\n    // Use ptr...\n\n    // Don't forget to free the allocated memory\n    free(ptr);\n\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, we allocate memory for a `TestType` object using `malloc` and check if the memory allocation was successful by comparing the returned pointer to `NULL`. If `malloc` fails, it returns `NULL` and we handle this case appropriately. Finally, we free the allocated memory using `free`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `stdlib.h` library for the `malloc` and `free` functions.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "7f7b1def-1b95-326a-83f2-cb63f64b3af3",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Prohibited C Function Found\" vulnerability refers to the use of certain C functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflow, format string vulnerabilities, or other security issues. In this case, the `fprintf` function is used, which can lead to format string vulnerabilities if the format string contains user-supplied data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using `fprintf` and similar functions that do not perform bounds checking. Instead, use safer alternatives that include bounds checking, such as `snprintf`. Also, never include user-supplied data in the format string of these functions.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `fprintf` function with the `snprintf` function, which includes bounds checking:\n\n```cpp\nchar buffer[256];\nsnprintf(buffer, sizeof(buffer), \"Something went wrong at i=%u, item is in wrong hash bucket: pNode->value=%u, numBins=%u\\n\",  i, pNode->value, numBins);\nfputs(buffer, stderr);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `stdio.h`: for `fprintf` and `stderr`\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-134: Use of Externally-Controlled Format String](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/134.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f4fc5416-4851-3ef9-babe-5111ccb415f3",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc()` function in C is used to dynamically allocate a block of memory. If the function fails to allocate the requested block of memory, a null pointer is returned. If the return value of `malloc()` is not checked, it may lead to a null pointer dereference, which can cause the program to crash or behave unpredictably. This is a common vulnerability in C programming, often referred to as \"Detected Vulnerability in C Function (malloc)\".\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always check the return value of `malloc()` before using the allocated memory. If `malloc()` returns a null pointer, handle the error appropriately to prevent the program from crashing or behaving unpredictably.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is an example of how to properly use `malloc()` and check its return value:\n\n```c++\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nvoid* ptr = malloc(numBytes);\nif (ptr == NULL) {\n    // Handle error\n} else {\n    // Use allocated memory\n    free(ptr);\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, if `malloc()` fails to allocate the requested block of memory, the error is handled appropriately.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc()` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional library dependencies are required.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d6a91b23-052a-33b4-96e4-3a614b32e1d9",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc()` function in C is used to dynamically allocate a block of memory. If the function fails to allocate the requested block of memory, a null pointer is returned. If the return value of `malloc()` is not checked, it may lead to a null pointer dereference, which can cause the program to crash or behave unpredictably. This is a common vulnerability in C programming, often referred to as \"Detected Vulnerability in C Function (malloc)\".\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always check the return value of `malloc()` before using the allocated memory. If `malloc()` returns a null pointer, handle the error appropriately to prevent the program from crashing or behaving unpredictably.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is an example of how to properly use `malloc()` and check its return value:\n\n```c++\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nvoid* ptr = malloc(numBytes);\nif (ptr == NULL) {\n    // Handle error\n} else {\n    // Use allocated memory\n    free(ptr);\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, if `malloc()` fails to allocate the requested block of memory, the error is handled appropriately.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc()` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional library dependencies are required.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)"
