            {
              "id": "69383267-d5ad-36eb-9c38-ceffdb513c7b",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc(Nbytes)` function in C++ is used to allocate a block of memory of specified size (Nbytes) and returns a pointer to the beginning of the block. The memory allocated by `malloc` is uninitialized and can contain any random data. If the space is insufficient, allocation fails and it returns a NULL pointer. \n\nA vulnerability can occur when the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated or controlled, leading to buffer overflow, memory corruption, or other related issues. This can potentially allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code or cause a Denial of Service (DoS) condition.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n1. Always validate and limit the size of the memory to be allocated. Avoid using user-supplied data directly in the `malloc` function without proper validation and sanitization.\n\n2. Check the return value of `malloc`. If it returns a NULL pointer, it means that the memory allocation has failed. The program should handle this condition properly to avoid null pointer dereferencing.\n\n3. Use functions that limit the amount of memory that can be written, such as `strncpy` instead of `strcpy`.\n\n4. Consider using modern C++ memory management techniques, such as smart pointers, which can automatically manage memory and help prevent memory leaks and dangling pointers.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```cpp\nchar *buffer = (char *) malloc(size);\nstrcpy(buffer, user_input);\n```\n\nUse:\n\n```cpp\nif (size > MAX_SIZE || size < MIN_SIZE) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n\nchar *buffer = (char *) malloc(size);\nif (buffer == NULL) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n\nstrncpy(buffer, user_input, size);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc` function is part of the C Standard Library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n\n## Matching CWE\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)\n- [CWE-680: Integer Overflow to Buffer Overflow](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/680.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "0c2f895d-fcff-366a-b069-c09f6449c6cc",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc()` function in C is used to dynamically allocate a block of memory. If the function fails to allocate the requested block of memory, a null pointer is returned. If the return value of `malloc()` is not checked, it may lead to a null pointer dereference, which can cause the program to crash or behave unpredictably. This is a common vulnerability in C programming, often referred to as \"Detected Vulnerability in C Function (malloc)\".\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always check the return value of `malloc()` before using the allocated memory. If `malloc()` returns a null pointer, handle the error appropriately to prevent the program from crashing or behaving unpredictably.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is an example of how to properly use `malloc()` and check its return value:\n\n```c++\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nvoid* ptr = malloc(numBytes);\nif (ptr == NULL) {\n    // Handle error\n} else {\n    // Use allocated memory\n    free(ptr);\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, if `malloc()` fails to allocate the requested block of memory, the error is handled appropriately.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc()` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional library dependencies are required.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d05700c8-d1c5-33c0-bc23-f9602a6ff48f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C++ is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. However, it can be a source of vulnerabilities if not used correctly. If the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated or controlled, it can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, memory corruption, or denial of service attacks.\n\nIn the given code snippet `malloc(sizeof(hipStream_t))`, the vulnerability could arise if the size of `hipStream_t` is not properly controlled or if the return value of `malloc` is not checked for null (which indicates that the memory allocation failed).\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n1. Always check the return value of `malloc` for null. If `malloc` returns null, it means that the memory allocation failed. Ignoring this can lead to null pointer dereferencing which can crash your program.\n\n2. Be careful with the size of the memory you are trying to allocate. If the size is user-controlled or can be influenced by an attacker, it can lead to buffer overflows or memory corruption.\n\n3. Consider using functions that limit the amount of memory that can be written such as `calloc` or `strncpy`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the given code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nhipStream_t* ptr = (hipStream_t*) malloc(sizeof(hipStream_t));\nif (ptr == NULL) {\n    // handle error\n} else {\n    // use ptr\n    free(ptr);\n}\n```\n\nIn this fixed code, we check if `malloc` returns null and handle the error appropriately. We also make sure to free the allocated memory after we are done using it to prevent memory leaks.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code snippet uses the `malloc` function which is part of the C standard library, so no additional library dependencies are required.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "901c256e-ae7b-3a84-9d8e-1f1051909fbb",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. However, it can be a source of vulnerabilities if not used correctly. If the size argument passed to `malloc` is controlled by an attacker, it can lead to a buffer overflow or underflow. This can result in arbitrary code execution, denial of service, or information disclosure.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n1. Always validate and sanitize input that is used to determine the size of the memory allocation.\n2. Check the return value of `malloc`. If it returns `NULL`, the memory allocation failed.\n3. Always initialize the allocated memory.\n4. Free the allocated memory when it is no longer needed to prevent memory leaks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```c\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\n// Assuming hPtr is a pointer to an integer\nint *hPtr = NULL;\n\n// Allocate memory\nhPtr = (int*)malloc(sizeof(*hPtr));\n\n// Check if memory allocation was successful\nif (hPtr == NULL) {\n    fprintf(stderr, \"Memory allocation failed!\\n\");\n    exit(1);\n}\n\n// Initialize memory\n*hPtr = 0;\n\n// Use hPtr...\n\n// Free allocated memory\nfree(hPtr);\nhPtr = NULL;\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `stdlib.h` library for the `malloc` and `free` functions.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "859e0807-3f71-35ba-8b4b-b0be31899744",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as 'Likely Vulnerable: malloc result used without null check (possible null deref)', and the call verdict is 'GENUINE', indicating a high-confidence, actionable issue. The malloc call assigns its result to 'hOutputData' and is immediately used as the destination in a memset call on the next line, without any check for a NULL return value. There is no zero guard or overflow guard present, and the first use is a memory operation (memset) that will dereference the pointer. This pattern is a classic source of null pointer dereference vulnerabilities, especially if malloc fails and returns NULL. The presence of a cast does not mitigate the risk. The pointer is freed in the method and also passed to another call, but these do not affect the immediate risk of dereferencing a NULL pointer. Therefore, this is a genuine vulnerability.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent a null pointer dereference after a failed memory allocation, always check the result of `malloc` before using the allocated memory. For example, if your original code is:\n\n```c\nhOutputData = (type *)malloc(size);\nmemset(hOutputData, 0, size);\n```\n\nYou should add a null check before using `hOutputData`:\n\n```c\nhOutputData = (type *)malloc(size);\nif (hOutputData == NULL) {\n    // Handle allocation failure, e.g., return error or cleanup\n    // ...\n} else {\n    memset(hOutputData, 0, size);\n}\n```\n\nThis ensures that if `malloc` fails and returns `NULL`, the program does not attempt to dereference a null pointer, which would lead to undefined behavior or a crash.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
