            {
              "id": "db4cb104-dd93-3601-a25d-2eb803a918a5",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as 'Likely Vulnerable: malloc result used without null check (possible null deref)', and the call verdict is 'GENUINE', indicating a high-confidence, actionable issue. The malloc call assigns its result to 'hOutputData' and is immediately used as the destination in a memset call on the next line, without any check for a NULL return value. There is no zero guard or overflow guard present, and the first use is a memory operation (memset) that will dereference the pointer. This pattern is a classic source of null pointer dereference vulnerabilities, especially if malloc fails and returns NULL. The presence of a cast does not mitigate the risk. The pointer is also stored in a field and passed to other calls, increasing the potential impact. Therefore, this is a genuine vulnerability.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent a null pointer dereference after a failed malloc, always check the result of malloc before using the allocated memory. For example, if your original code is:\n\n```c\nhOutputData = (type *)malloc(size);\nmemset(hOutputData, 0, size);\n```\n\nYou should add a null check before using `hOutputData`:\n\n```c\nhOutputData = (type *)malloc(size);\nif (hOutputData == NULL) {\n    // Handle allocation failure, e.g., return error, cleanup, or log\n    // return or exit as appropriate\n}\nmemset(hOutputData, 0, size);\n```\n\nThis ensures that if malloc fails and returns NULL, the program does not attempt to dereference a null pointer, which would lead to undefined behavior or a crash.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a3abe2c3-e295-3169-bf96-51973ee7c23d",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as 'Likely Vulnerable: malloc result used without null check (possible null deref)', and the call verdict is 'GENUINE', indicating a high-confidence, actionable issue. The code allocates memory using malloc with a cast, but there is no check to ensure that the allocation succeeded before the pointer is used (first use is a pointer field access, which is dangerous). There is no null guard before the first use, and no zero or overflow guard is present. These factors strongly suggest a real risk of null pointer dereference if malloc fails, which can lead to undefined behavior or crashes. The presence of a cast does not mitigate the risk. The pointer is also passed to another call, but this does not reduce the risk in this context.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nAlways check the result of malloc before using the allocated pointer. If malloc returns NULL, handle the error appropriately (e.g., return an error code, log the failure, or clean up). For example, if your original code is:\n\n```c\nHIP_ARRAY3D_DESCRIPTOR *desc = (HIP_ARRAY3D_DESCRIPTOR *)malloc(sizeof(HIP_ARRAY3D_DESCRIPTOR));\ndesc->Width = ...; // unsafe if malloc failed\n```\n\nA safe version would be:\n\n```c\nHIP_ARRAY3D_DESCRIPTOR *desc = (HIP_ARRAY3D_DESCRIPTOR *)malloc(sizeof(HIP_ARRAY3D_DESCRIPTOR));\nif (desc == NULL) {\n    // Handle allocation failure (e.g., return, log, or cleanup)\n    return;\n}\ndesc->Width = ...; // safe to use desc\n```\n\nThis prevents undefined behavior or crashes due to null pointer dereference if memory allocation fails.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "341cbcbb-b997-3f55-9a04-b4c2c89e9806",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as 'Likely Vulnerable: malloc result used without null check (possible null deref)', and the call verdict is 'GENUINE', indicating a high-confidence, actionable issue. The code allocates memory using malloc and immediately uses the result as the destination in a memset call without checking if the allocation succeeded (i.e., if the returned pointer is not NULL). There is no zero guard or overflow guard present, and the first use is a memory operation (memset) that will dereference the pointer, which is dangerous if malloc fails. The presence of a cast does not mitigate the risk. The pointer is also stored in a field and freed in the method, but these do not address the immediate risk of null dereference. Therefore, this is a genuine vulnerability and not a false positive.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nAlways check the result of malloc before using the allocated memory. If malloc returns NULL, handle the error appropriately (e.g., by returning an error code, logging, or cleaning up). For example, if your original code is:\n\n```c\nhOutputData = (type *)malloc(size);\nmemset(hOutputData, 0, size);\n```\n\nA safe remediation would be:\n\n```c\nhOutputData = (type *)malloc(size);\nif (hOutputData == NULL) {\n    // Handle allocation failure (e.g., return error, log, cleanup)\n    return ERROR_CODE;\n}\nmemset(hOutputData, 0, size);\n```\n\nThis ensures that memset is only called if the allocation succeeded, preventing a possible null pointer dereference.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "9edb4dac-95b7-35fd-8e0b-3808912a8e3d",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc(Nbytes)` function in C++ is used to allocate a block of memory of specified size (Nbytes) and returns a pointer to the beginning of the block. The memory allocated by `malloc` is uninitialized and can contain any random data. If the space is insufficient, allocation fails and it returns a NULL pointer. \n\nA vulnerability can occur when the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated or controlled, leading to buffer overflow, memory corruption, or other related issues. This can potentially allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code or cause a Denial of Service (DoS) condition.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n1. Always validate and limit the size of the memory to be allocated. Avoid using user-supplied data directly in the `malloc` function without proper validation and sanitization.\n\n2. Check the return value of `malloc`. If it returns a NULL pointer, it means that the memory allocation has failed. The program should handle this condition properly to avoid null pointer dereferencing.\n\n3. Use functions that limit the amount of memory that can be written, such as `strncpy` instead of `strcpy`.\n\n4. Consider using modern C++ memory management techniques, such as smart pointers, which can automatically manage memory and help prevent memory leaks and dangling pointers.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```cpp\nchar *buffer = (char *) malloc(size);\nstrcpy(buffer, user_input);\n```\n\nUse:\n\n```cpp\nif (size > MAX_SIZE || size < MIN_SIZE) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n\nchar *buffer = (char *) malloc(size);\nif (buffer == NULL) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n\nstrncpy(buffer, user_input, size);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc` function is part of the C Standard Library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n\n## Matching CWE\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)\n- [CWE-680: Integer Overflow to Buffer Overflow](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/680.html)"
