                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc()` function in C is used to dynamically allocate a block of memory. If the function fails to allocate the requested block of memory, a null pointer is returned. If the return value of `malloc()` is not checked, it may lead to a null pointer dereference, which can cause the program to crash or behave unpredictably. This is a common vulnerability in C programming, often referred to as \"Detected Vulnerability in C Function (malloc)\".\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always check the return value of `malloc()` before using the allocated memory. If `malloc()` returns a null pointer, handle the error appropriately to prevent the program from crashing or behaving unpredictably.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is an example of how to properly use `malloc()` and check its return value:\n\n```c++\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nvoid* ptr = malloc(numBytes);\nif (ptr == NULL) {\n    // Handle error\n} else {\n    // Use allocated memory\n    free(ptr);\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, if `malloc()` fails to allocate the requested block of memory, the error is handled appropriately.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc()` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional library dependencies are required.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)"
