              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as 'Likely Vulnerable: malloc result used without null check (possible null deref)', which indicates a high-confidence issue. The malloc call assigns its result to 'hOutputData', but there is no null check before the first use of this pointer in a memset call. The absence of a zero guard or overflow guard further increases the risk, as does the immediate use of the pointer as a destination in a memory operation. The presence of a cast does not mitigate the risk. Although the pointer is freed in the method and passed to another call, these do not address the core issue of a potential null dereference. Therefore, this is a genuine vulnerability where a failed allocation could lead to undefined behavior or a crash.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent a null pointer dereference after a failed memory allocation, always check the result of malloc before using the pointer. For example, after:\n\n```c\nhOutputData = (type *)malloc(size);\n```\nadd a null check before using `hOutputData`:\n\n```c\nhOutputData = (type *)malloc(size);\nif (hOutputData == NULL) {\n    // Handle allocation failure, e.g., return error or clean up\n    fprintf(stderr, \"Memory allocation failed\\n\");\n    return;\n}\nmemset(hOutputData, 0, size);\n```\nThis ensures that if malloc fails and returns NULL, the program does not attempt to use the null pointer, preventing undefined behavior or a crash.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "3d518307-c6eb-395d-bd1e-bc86bba851d8",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as 'Likely Vulnerable: malloc result used without null check (possible null deref)', and the call verdict is 'GENUINE', indicating a high-confidence, actionable issue. The code allocates memory using malloc and immediately uses the result as the destination in a memset call without checking if the allocation succeeded (i.e., if the returned pointer is not NULL). There is no zero guard or overflow guard present, and the first use is a memory operation (memset) that would dereference the pointer. This pattern can lead to a null pointer dereference if malloc fails, which is a common and serious bug in C/C++ code. The presence of a cast does not mitigate the risk. The pointer is freed in the method, but this does not affect the immediate risk of dereferencing a null pointer.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nAlways check the result of malloc before using the allocated memory. If malloc returns NULL, handle the error appropriately (e.g., by returning from the function, logging an error, or cleaning up resources). For example, if your original code is:\n\n```c\nhInputData = (type *)malloc(size);\nmemset(hInputData, 0, size);\n```\n\nA safe remediation would be:\n\n```c\nhInputData = (type *)malloc(size);\nif (hInputData == NULL) {\n    // Handle allocation failure (e.g., return error, cleanup, etc.)\n    return;\n}\nmemset(hInputData, 0, size);\n```\n\nThis ensures that memset is only called if the allocation succeeded, preventing a possible null pointer dereference.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f56ab45b-627f-3599-9b5b-0d94d7bb6b91",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc()` function in C++ is used to dynamically allocate a block of memory. If the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated, it can lead to several vulnerabilities such as buffer overflow, memory corruption, or denial of service. This is because an attacker can provide a large value for `memsize` that can cause the program to allocate more memory than is available, leading to a crash or other unexpected behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate the size of the memory to be allocated. This includes checking that the size is not negative and that it does not exceed the maximum allowable size. You should also handle the case where `malloc()` fails to allocate the requested memory, which returns a null pointer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the `malloc(memsize)` vulnerability:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nif (memsize <= 0 || memsize > MAX_SIZE) {\n    // Handle error: invalid size\n} else {\n    void* ptr = malloc(memsize);\n    if (ptr == NULL) {\n        // Handle error: memory allocation failed\n    } else {\n        // Use allocated memory\n        free(ptr);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `MAX_SIZE` should be replaced with the maximum size of memory that your program should allocate.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc()` function is part of the C standard library, so you need to include `stdlib.h` to use it.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a6f5547b-99bb-3ab4-bfca-8361b18ed714",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc()` function in C++ is used to dynamically allocate a block of memory. If the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated, it can lead to several vulnerabilities such as buffer overflow, memory corruption, or denial of service. This is because an attacker can provide a large value for `memsize` that can cause the program to allocate more memory than is available, leading to a crash or other unexpected behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate the size of the memory to be allocated. This includes checking that the size is not negative and that it does not exceed the maximum allowable size. You should also handle the case where `malloc()` fails to allocate the requested memory, which returns a null pointer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the `malloc(memsize)` vulnerability:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nif (memsize <= 0 || memsize > MAX_SIZE) {\n    // Handle error: invalid size\n} else {\n    void* ptr = malloc(memsize);\n    if (ptr == NULL) {\n        // Handle error: memory allocation failed\n    } else {\n        // Use allocated memory\n        free(ptr);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `MAX_SIZE` should be replaced with the maximum size of memory that your program should allocate.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc()` function is part of the C standard library, so you need to include `stdlib.h` to use it.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
              },
