            {
              "id": "dc2cbd95-dc87-3d33-9572-1839e3ba5080",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. The vulnerability arises when the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated or calculated, leading to buffer overflow, memory corruption, or other related issues. In the given code snippet, `malloc(sizeof(int))`, there is no immediate vulnerability. However, if the size of the memory to be allocated is determined based on user input or other untrusted data, it can lead to vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n1. Always validate and sanitize the size of the memory to be allocated. Never trust user input or other untrusted data.\n2. Consider using functions that limit the amount of memory allocated, such as `calloc`.\n3. Use modern C++ features like smart pointers (`std::unique_ptr`, `std::shared_ptr`) to manage dynamic memory, which can help prevent memory leaks and other related issues.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIf the size of the memory to be allocated is determined based on user input, validate and sanitize the input. For example:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nint* allocateArray(int size) {\n    if (size <= 0) {\n        // Handle error\n        return NULL;\n    }\n\n    int* array = (int*) malloc(size * sizeof(int));\n    if (array == NULL) {\n        // Handle error\n        return NULL;\n    }\n\n    return array;\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional library dependencies are required.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f253ef84-897b-35c4-b503-9ca7d053745a",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc` function in C++. This function is used to allocate a block of memory dynamically. The size of this block is determined by the argument passed to the function. If the argument is a user-controlled value, it can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflow, integer overflow, or even denial of service (DoS) attacks.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `devcount * sizeof(hipGraphExec_t)` could potentially lead to an integer overflow if `devcount` is a large value. This can result in a smaller block of memory being allocated than expected, leading to buffer overflow vulnerabilities when this memory is used.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate and limit the size of the memory being allocated. Avoid using user-controlled values directly in memory allocation functions like `malloc`. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nA simple fix would be to check if an overflow would occur before calling `malloc`. Here is an example:\n\n```cpp\nif (devcount > SIZE_MAX / sizeof(hipGraphExec_t)) {\n    // handle error: the requested size is too large\n} else {\n    hipGraphExec_t* ptr = (hipGraphExec_t*) malloc(devcount * sizeof(hipGraphExec_t));\n    if (ptr == NULL) {\n        // handle error: malloc failed\n    } else {\n        // use ptr\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example provided does not indicate any specific library dependencies. However, the `malloc` function is part of the C standard library, so the code would need to include `stdlib.h`.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)\n- [CWE-680: Integer Overflow to Buffer Overflow](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/680.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc56f598-6317-361d-a5a4-7452e8b2b39e",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. The vulnerability arises when the size of the memory to be allocated is not correctly calculated, leading to buffer overflow or underflow errors. In the provided code snippet, `malloc(sizeof(int*))` is allocating memory of size equal to the size of an integer pointer, which may not be the intended size for the data to be stored. This can lead to memory corruption, data leakage, or even execution of arbitrary code if exploited.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n1. Always calculate the size of the memory to be allocated correctly. Consider the type and number of elements to be stored.\n2. Check the return value of `malloc`. If it returns `NULL`, the memory allocation failed.\n3. Always free the allocated memory after use to prevent memory leaks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIf you intend to allocate memory for an integer, use `sizeof(int)` instead of `sizeof(int*)`. If you want to allocate memory for multiple integers, multiply the size of an integer by the number of elements.\n\n```c++\nint* ptr = (int*) malloc(n * sizeof(int)); // where n is the number of elements\nif (ptr == NULL) {\n    // handle memory allocation failure\n}\n// use ptr\nfree(ptr); // free memory after use\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc` function is part of the C Standard Library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c339cdae-6f7a-39bd-9673-1ff2d466f6ef",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. The vulnerability arises when the size of the memory to be allocated is not correctly calculated, leading to buffer overflow or underflow errors. In the provided code snippet, `malloc(sizeof(int*))` is allocating memory of size equal to the size of an integer pointer, which may not be the intended size for the data to be stored. This can lead to memory corruption, data leakage, or even execution of arbitrary code if exploited.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n1. Always calculate the size of the memory to be allocated correctly. Consider the type and number of elements to be stored.\n2. Check the return value of `malloc`. If it returns `NULL`, the memory allocation failed.\n3. Always free the allocated memory after use to prevent memory leaks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIf you intend to allocate memory for an integer, use `sizeof(int)` instead of `sizeof(int*)`. If you want to allocate memory for multiple integers, multiply the size of an integer by the number of elements.\n\n```c++\nint* ptr = (int*) malloc(n * sizeof(int)); // where n is the number of elements\nif (ptr == NULL) {\n    // handle memory allocation failure\n}\n// use ptr\nfree(ptr); // free memory after use\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc` function is part of the C Standard Library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
              },
