            {
              "id": "0768a5e0-c239-39e4-b7ad-2f9397e4f6a0",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C++ is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. The vulnerability arises when the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated or calculated, leading to buffer overflow, memory corruption, or other related issues. In the given code snippet, `malloc(sizeof(float))`, there is no immediate vulnerability unless the returned pointer is misused. However, it's worth noting that `malloc` does not initialize the memory it allocates, which can lead to undefined behavior if the memory is read before being written.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n1. Always check the return value of `malloc`. If `malloc` fails to allocate the requested block of memory, it returns a null pointer, and any attempt to use this null pointer will likely cause the program to crash.\n\n2. Always initialize the memory allocated by `malloc` before reading it.\n\n3. Use `calloc` instead of `malloc` if you want the allocated memory to be initialized to zero.\n\n4. Always free the memory allocated by `malloc` when it is no longer needed to prevent memory leaks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\nfloat* ptr = (float*) malloc(sizeof(float));\nif (ptr == NULL) {\n    // handle error\n} else {\n    *ptr = 0.0f; // initialize memory\n    // use ptr\n    free(ptr); // free memory when done\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c37ceef3-e0f4-3eaa-97d8-5640f69b0bfe",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. The vulnerability arises when the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated or calculated, leading to buffer overflow, integer overflow, or other memory corruption issues. In the given code snippet `malloc( N * sizeof(char)`, if the value of `N` is not properly validated, it can lead to these issues.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n1. Always validate the size of memory to be allocated. Ensure that it is within the acceptable range and not a negative value.\n2. Use functions that limit the amount of data written into a buffer such as `strncpy` instead of `strcpy`.\n3. Consider using modern C++ features and libraries that manage memory automatically, such as smart pointers or container classes like `std::vector`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n// Check if N is within acceptable range\nif (N > 0 && N <= MAX_SIZE) {\n    char* buffer = (char*) malloc(N * sizeof(char));\n    if (buffer == NULL) {\n        // Handle malloc failure\n    }\n    // Use buffer\n    free(buffer);\n} else {\n    // Handle invalid size\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `stdlib.h` library for the `malloc` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n\n## Matching CWE\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)\n- [CWE-680: Integer Overflow to Buffer Overflow](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/680.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "59e86b18-6855-3063-8e43-9581627a18f8",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. The vulnerability arises when the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated or calculated, leading to buffer overflow, integer overflow, or other memory corruption issues. In the given code snippet `malloc( N * sizeof(char)`, if the value of `N` is not properly validated, it can lead to these issues.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n1. Always validate the size of memory to be allocated. Ensure that it is within the acceptable range and not a negative value.\n2. Use functions that limit the amount of data written into a buffer such as `strncpy` instead of `strcpy`.\n3. Consider using modern C++ features and libraries that manage memory automatically, such as smart pointers or container classes like `std::vector`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n// Check if N is within acceptable range\nif (N > 0 && N <= MAX_SIZE) {\n    char* buffer = (char*) malloc(N * sizeof(char));\n    if (buffer == NULL) {\n        // Handle malloc failure\n    }\n    // Use buffer\n    free(buffer);\n} else {\n    // Handle invalid size\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `stdlib.h` library for the `malloc` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n\n## Matching CWE\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)\n- [CWE-680: Integer Overflow to Buffer Overflow](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/680.html)"
