              "id": "43d3fe0e-5294-3f79-b321-eabbf7244453",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as 'Likely Vulnerable: malloc result used without null check (possible null deref)', and the call verdict is 'GENUINE', indicating a high-confidence, actionable issue. The malloc call assigns its result to 'snapp', but there is no null guard before the first use, which is as a destination in a memcpy operation. This means that if malloc fails and returns NULL, the subsequent use of 'snapp' could result in a null pointer dereference, leading to undefined behavior or a crash. The absence of zero guards, overflow guards, and a null check before use all increase the risk. The presence of a cast does not mitigate the risk. The pointer is freed in the method and passed to another call, but these do not affect the core vulnerability. Therefore, this is a genuine vulnerability that should be addressed.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nAlways check the result of malloc for NULL before using the allocated memory. If malloc fails, handle the error appropriately (e.g., by returning an error code, cleaning up, or aborting the operation). For example, if your original code is:\n\n```c\nsnapp = (type *)malloc(size);\nmemcpy(snapp, src, size);\n```\n\nA safe remediation would be:\n\n```c\nsnapp = (type *)malloc(size);\nif (snapp == NULL) {\n    // Handle allocation failure (log, cleanup, return error, etc.)\n    // Example:\n    fprintf(stderr, \"Memory allocation failed\\n\");\n    return ERROR_CODE;\n}\nmemcpy(snapp, src, size);\n```\n\nThis ensures that you do not dereference a NULL pointer if the allocation fails.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "73aa12d5-687c-39eb-9e93-946fc7218c40",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as 'Likely Vulnerable: malloc result used without null check (possible null deref)', and the call verdict is 'GENUINE', indicating a high-confidence, actionable issue. The code allocates memory using malloc(size) and assigns it to the variable 'snap', but there is no null check before the first use of 'snap' as the destination in a memcpy call. This means that if malloc fails and returns NULL, the subsequent use of 'snap' could result in a null pointer dereference, leading to undefined behavior or a crash. There are no zero guards, overflow guards, or null guards present before the first use, and the first use is a memory operation (memcpy), which is considered dangerous. The presence of a cast does not mitigate the risk. The pointer is freed in the method, but this does not affect the risk of dereferencing a null pointer. Therefore, this is a genuine vulnerability that should be addressed.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nAlways check the result of malloc before using the allocated memory. If malloc returns NULL, handle the error appropriately (e.g., by returning an error code, cleaning up, or aborting the operation). For example, after the allocation:\n\n```c\nsnap = (type *)malloc(size);\nif (snap == NULL) {\n    // Handle allocation failure: log, cleanup, return error, etc.\n    // Example:\n    fprintf(stderr, \"Memory allocation failed\\n\");\n    return;\n}\n```\nThis ensures that if malloc fails, the program does not attempt to use a null pointer, preventing undefined behavior or crashes.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "0dd36c9b-a0bf-3cb9-844b-72c151a2220a",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memory copy operation. The destination and source are both pointers, and dataflow analysis suggests that they may refer to overlapping memory areas. Additionally, there is no evidence of a guard to prevent this, and the source pointer may also be null at this point, which could lead to a crash or further undefined behavior. The classification and verdict both indicate a high-confidence issue that should be addressed to prevent potential memory corruption or program instability.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo remediate the risk of undefined behavior caused by possible overlapping memory regions in the memory copy operation, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap. If overlap is possible, replace the memory copy function with a function that safely handles overlapping regions, such as `memmove`. Additionally, add explicit checks to ensure that the source pointer is not null before performing the copy operation.\n\nExample fix:\n\n```cpp\nif (snap != nullptr && Eh != nullptr) {\n    // Use memmove instead of memcpy if overlap is possible\n    memmove(snap, Eh, size);\n}\n```\n\nThis change ensures that the operation is safe even if the memory regions overlap and prevents null pointer dereference.\n\n## References\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "520cbb0b-92e0-36af-8cff-f64d79e15a62",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as 'Likely Vulnerable: malloc result used without null check (possible null deref)', and the call verdict is 'GENUINE', indicating a high-confidence, actionable issue. The malloc call assigns its result to 'snap', which is then used as the destination in a memcpy operation without a preceding null check. There is no zero guard or overflow guard present, and the first use is a memory operation (memcpy) that would dereference the pointer if allocation failed. The presence of a cast does not mitigate the risk. Although the pointer is freed in the method and passed to another call, these do not address the core vulnerability. Therefore, this is a genuine risk of null pointer dereference after a failed allocation.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent a null pointer dereference after a failed allocation, always check the result of malloc before using the allocated memory. For example, if your original code is:\n\n```c\nsnap = (type *)malloc(size);\nmemcpy(snap, src, size);\n```\n\nYou should add a null check after the allocation and before any use:\n\n```c\nsnap = (type *)malloc(size);\nif (snap == NULL) {\n    // Handle allocation failure, e.g., return error or clean up\n    // ...\n    return;\n}\nmemcpy(snap, src, size);\n```\n\nThis ensures that if malloc fails and returns NULL, the program will not attempt to dereference a null pointer, preventing undefined behavior or a crash.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "0a8cd9e8-301b-3339-aaa6-6723b2ddeb90",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc()` function in C is used to dynamically allocate a block of memory. If the function fails to allocate the requested block of memory, a null pointer is returned. If the return value of `malloc()` is not checked, it may lead to a null pointer dereference, which can cause the program to crash or behave unpredictably. This is a common vulnerability in C programming, often referred to as \"Detected Vulnerability in C Function (malloc)\".\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always check the return value of `malloc()` before using the allocated memory. If `malloc()` returns a null pointer, handle the error appropriately to prevent the program from crashing or behaving unpredictably.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is an example of how to properly use `malloc()` and check its return value:\n\n```c++\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nvoid* ptr = malloc(numBytes);\nif (ptr == NULL) {\n    // Handle error\n} else {\n    // Use allocated memory\n    free(ptr);\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, if `malloc()` fails to allocate the requested block of memory, the error is handled appropriately.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc()` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional library dependencies are required.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "b8f34266-4e96-3e62-8969-9c02938e50d2",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc(Nbytes)` function in C++ is used to allocate a block of memory of specified size (Nbytes) and returns a pointer to the beginning of the block. The memory allocated by `malloc` is uninitialized and can contain any random data. If the space is insufficient, allocation fails and it returns a NULL pointer. \n\nA vulnerability can occur when the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated or controlled, leading to buffer overflow, memory corruption, or other related issues. This can potentially allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code or cause a Denial of Service (DoS) condition.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n1. Always validate and limit the size of the memory to be allocated. Avoid using user-supplied data directly in the `malloc` function without proper validation and sanitization.\n\n2. Check the return value of `malloc`. If it returns a NULL pointer, it means that the memory allocation has failed. The program should handle this condition properly to avoid null pointer dereferencing.\n\n3. Use functions that limit the amount of memory that can be written, such as `strncpy` instead of `strcpy`.\n\n4. Consider using modern C++ memory management techniques, such as smart pointers, which can automatically manage memory and help prevent memory leaks and dangling pointers.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```cpp\nchar *buffer = (char *) malloc(size);\nstrcpy(buffer, user_input);\n```\n\nUse:\n\n```cpp\nif (size > MAX_SIZE || size < MIN_SIZE) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n\nchar *buffer = (char *) malloc(size);\nif (buffer == NULL) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n\nstrncpy(buffer, user_input, size);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc` function is part of the C Standard Library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n\n## Matching CWE\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)\n- [CWE-680: Integer Overflow to Buffer Overflow](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/680.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "33b6e4d1-4237-3856-afa1-d61db21113e6",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc(Nbytes)` function in C++ is used to allocate a block of memory of specified size (Nbytes) and returns a pointer to the beginning of the block. The memory allocated by `malloc` is uninitialized and can contain any random data. If the space is insufficient, allocation fails and it returns a NULL pointer. \n\nA vulnerability can occur when the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated or controlled, leading to buffer overflow, memory corruption, or other related issues. This can potentially allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code or cause a Denial of Service (DoS) condition.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n1. Always validate and limit the size of the memory to be allocated. Avoid using user-supplied data directly in the `malloc` function without proper validation and sanitization.\n\n2. Check the return value of `malloc`. If it returns a NULL pointer, it means that the memory allocation has failed. The program should handle this condition properly to avoid null pointer dereferencing.\n\n3. Use functions that limit the amount of memory that can be written, such as `strncpy` instead of `strcpy`.\n\n4. Consider using modern C++ memory management techniques, such as smart pointers, which can automatically manage memory and help prevent memory leaks and dangling pointers.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```cpp\nchar *buffer = (char *) malloc(size);\nstrcpy(buffer, user_input);\n```\n\nUse:\n\n```cpp\nif (size > MAX_SIZE || size < MIN_SIZE) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n\nchar *buffer = (char *) malloc(size);\nif (buffer == NULL) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n\nstrncpy(buffer, user_input, size);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc` function is part of the C Standard Library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n\n## Matching CWE\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)\n- [CWE-680: Integer Overflow to Buffer Overflow](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/680.html)"
